==English==

===Alternative spellings===
* [[Cubism]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr}} {{term|cubisme|lang=fr}}. One story is that, in 1908, as a new canvas by [[w:George Braque|Braques]] was being carried past, someone said, “''Encore des Cubes! assez de cubisme!''”.  The quotations below ascribe the coinage to Matisse.

See also the word [[cube]] (from {{etyl|la}} {{term|cubus|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc}} {{term|κύβος|sc=polytonic|tr=kybos|lang=grc}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkjuːb.izəm/}} {{SAMPA|/"kjU:b.iz@m/}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{context|often|capitalized}} An [[artistic]] [[movement]] in the early 20th Century characterized by the depiction of [[natural]] [[form]]s as [[geometric]] structures of [[plane]]s.

====Quotations====
* '''2003''', ''The New Yorker'', 3 March,
*: Matisse coined the name '''Cubism''' as a derisive joke.
* '''2005''', ''The New Yorker'', 29 Aug, p. 78,
*: A few recall that, in 1908, he [Matisse] inspired the coinage of the term '''“cubism,”''' in disparagement of a movement that would eclipse his leading influence on the Parisian avant-garde.

====Related terms====
* [[cubist]]
* [[cubistic]]
* [[cubistically]]

